      chapterTitle: "Magic Baloney",
      pages: "239–242",
      pdfPages: "264–267",
      title: "Magic Baloney: start by hating your product, then let it win you over",
      minutes: 8,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 28 · Magic Baloney · pp. 239–242 (PDF 264–267)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "I brought out the worst aspects of this product and turned them around.", page: "239" },
        { text: "First I realized that the number one drawback in purchasing a thermostat is the installation.", page: "239" }
      ],
      idea: `**The ad in brief.** A JS&A ad for the Magic Stat programmable thermostat. The headline is "Magic Baloney." The subheadline promises you'll enjoy how much JS&A hated the thing until something amazing happened, and the photo caption trashes it: no digital readout, an ugly case, a silly name. The story has a twist: JS&A starts out disgusted and ends up calling it the best thermostat ever made. It ran almost three years from 1983, made Magic Stat a brand, and the company was later sold to Honeywell. Price: $79, or $99 deluxe.

**What Sugarman points out:**
1. **Find the number one drawback and hit it early** (second quote). Installation scares buyers: wires, danger, the cost of an installer. So the first good feature the story "discovers" is a few-minute, color-matched, low-voltage install with no serviceman (copy elements #8 and #9; trigger #4 Credibility).
2. **Raise every objection the buyer will have and resolve them one at a time:** the case, the look, the name (copy element #8). In Chapter 18 he uses this ad as his example of raising the design objection at the very beginning.
3. **Explain features lightly.** Much of the copy walks through the features in a breezy, conversational way.
4. **Build up the maker near the end.** The competition was Honeywell, an established name, so JS&A says it checked out the Magic Stat company to make buyers feel secure (triggers #4 Credibility, #8 Establish Authority).
5. **Knocking the product creates curiosity.** Readers wonder what the gimmick is and why he's knocking it, and they start reading (trigger #19; Axiom 8). Chapter 8 adds that the clean layout set the environment, and the tone was flip, sarcastic and skeptical.
6. **After the install, it's all downhill.** Once the hardest obstacle is out of the way, the remaining features, objections and benefits slide by (Axiom 6).

**More to notice (his checklist applied):**
- Capitalized paragraph headings move you from "loser" to testing it to savings (copy element #4). A skeptical "so what?" followed by an invitation to keep reading carries you over a slow spot (Axiom 8).
- A walk through a full day of temperature changes puts the reader in his own house (triggers #1, #29).
- Value and justification: energy savings, a tax credit, the maker's buy-back if the unit doesn't pay for itself in a year, and JS&A's own one-year return (triggers #5, #6, #9).
- The deluxe model's nicer case quietly answers the ugly-case objection, and a closing joke about the name answers the last one with humor (trigger #25).

**The lesson** is the first quote. Say the worst things about your product first and honestly, and they become the hook.`,
