      pdfPages: "276–279",
      title: "Hungarian Conspiracy: tell readers not to buy it, and make them curious enough to order",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 31 · Hungarian Conspiracy · pp. 251–254 (PDF 276–279)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "The environment created in the ad for the product is both a story and a tease.", page: "251" },
        { text: "This was a really good example of pushing the envelope.", page: "254" }
      ],
      idea: `**The ad in brief.** A 1983 JS&A ad for the XL-25, a handheld light puzzle: a 5×5 grid of lighted buttons where each press flips the lights around it, and the goal is to light them all in the fewest presses. It ran only in JS&A's catalog and was moderately successful, but it was one of Sugarman's favorites. He traveled to Hungary, did the photography in Budapest and met Erno Rubik, inventor of the Rubik's Cube.

The whole ad is a tongue-in-cheek "exclusive report from Budapest," styled as a smuggled Cold War intelligence dispatch. Its mock theory: the Rubik's Cube kept Americans so busy twisting that productivity fell and a recession followed, so now the communists want Hungary to flood America with addictive games. It introduces the three designers with spy-thriller captions, explains the puzzle, notes an American chip maker and a Hong Kong factory, and admits JS&A got hooked and bought thousands. Only in the last paragraph is the product actually offered (about $29): buy one, don't play with it, and use it to warn your neighbors. The ad ends by calling anyone who buys one an idiot.

**What Sugarman points out:**
1. **A unique message: readers are urged not to buy,** with a tongue-in-cheek case that the product could cause another recession.
2. **The environment is a story and a tease** (first quote). "What's the gimmick if they aren't selling the product?" keeps you slipping through the copy (trigger #26 Storytelling; trigger #19 Curiosity; Axiom 6).
3. **The answer waits until the last paragraph.** Curiosity carries the entire ad (Axiom 8; Chapter 10's seeds of curiosity on a large scale).
4. **Pushing the envelope** (second quote). Even insulting the buyer worked, because the copy was strong enough to get read and the curiosity strong enough to make people order.

**More to notice:**
- **Linking and timing.** The ad borrows the Rubik's Cube fad and Cold War paranoia, things readers in 1983 already knew and felt (triggers #12 Current Fads, #13 Timing, #14 Linking).
- **The product explanation survives the joke.** You learn how the game works and who built it, so logic is there to justify the purchase (copy element #5; Emotion Principle 3).
- **Mental engagement.** Readers have to work out that it's a joke and what's really on offer, which makes the ad more enjoyable (trigger #27).
- **The right audience.** It ran to JS&A's own catalog readers, who knew the voice and were in on the joke (triggers #30 Familiarity, #11 Prospect Nature).

**The lesson:** a story and a tease can carry a product all the way to the last line, as long as the product, the audience and the moment can take the joke.`,
      keyPoints: [
        "Telling readers not to buy can work when the joke is obvious and the curiosity is real",
        "Story + tease: hold back the answer to “what's for sale?” until the end",
        "Link the product to what the public already knows and feels right now (fad, fear, news)",
        "Keep the real product explanation inside the joke so buyers can justify the purchase",
        "Save envelope-pushing ads for an audience that knows your voice (it ran in the catalog)"
      ],
