      id: "aw-033",
      part: "s3",
      chapter: 33,
      chapterTitle: "Gold Space Chains",
      pages: "259–261",
      pdfPages: "284–286",
      title: "Gold Space Chains: the reluctant-seller story, and the cost of offending part of your audience",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 33 · Gold Space Chains · pp. 259–261 (PDF 284–286)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Notice the use of the editorial “we” in the ad copy. It would have been more personal had I used the word “I” instead.", page: "259" },
        { text: "It is a good example of the insensitivity to women’s issues that I had at the time.", page: "259" }
      ],
      idea: `**The ad in brief.** A 1978 JS&A catalog ad for 14-karat gold chains, a product with nothing to do with space-age electronics. The headline is "Gold Space Chains." The copy is a comic story in the editorial "we." Bob, a persistent jewelry rep and old friend, keeps pitching gold chains, and JS&A keeps refusing on principle: space-age products only, the deadline has passed, a reputation to protect. Bob tries angle after angle: a gift for the women in mostly male customers' lives, readers wealthy and smart enough to spot value, a space-age name. Then he pulls out a photo of his attractive cousin, who could model the chains, and JS&A suddenly decides the idea has merit. The chains are priced below jewelry stores, readers are invited to compare, and the ad ends by teasing Bob.

**What Sugarman points out:**
1. **The premise.** A product that didn't belong in the catalog needed a powerful reason to be there. The comic reason was the model.
2. **It offended many women.** He calls it an example of his insensitivity to women's issues at the time (second quote) and says he learned. He challenges you to find the passage that might offend a woman working in a mostly male field. In Chapter 18 (copy element #10 Gender) he quotes a furious two-page letter from a reader who listed women's achievements in technical fields, the military and air traffic control, and wished him bankruptcy. Know your audience and never write anything that offends a group.
3. **The editorial "we."** "I" would have been more personal (first quote; Axiom 12).
4. **Timing, not just copy.** Gold prices were low when the catalog went out, then shot up. Response grew and the chains sold out. He credits the success mostly to the serendipity of timing (trigger #13).
5. **It still drew praise.** Plenty of readers wrote to congratulate him on entertaining, creative copy.

**More to notice:**
- **The reluctant-seller structure.** Each refusal shows the seller's standards (trigger #3 Integrity), and each of Bob's arguments plants a buying reason (a gift, a bargain, smart customers) without JS&A ever pushing it. Readers draw the conclusion themselves (trigger #27 Mental Engagement).
- **Linking.** The "space" name ties an unrelated product to the brand, and the story pokes fun at doing exactly that (trigger #14).
- **Flattering the prospect** as affluent and smart (triggers #11 Prospect Nature, #17 Desire to Belong).
- **A price comparison** you're invited to make, even after delivery (copy element #17; trigger #5).

**The lesson:** entertaining copy can sell and still alienate part of the people reading it. And an ad that wins because the market moved isn't proof the copy was right.`,
