      id: "aw-034",
      part: "s3",
      chapter: 34,
      chapterTitle: "Consumers Hero",
      pages: "263–266",
      pdfPages: "288–291",
      title: "Consumers Hero: the slippery slide, written in the customer's own language",
      minutes: 8,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 34 · Consumers Hero · pp. 263–266 (PDF 288–291)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "A good copywriter can write to fit any market.", page: "266" },
        { text: "It was also a good example of relating in a human way without being humorous.", page: "263" }
      ],
      idea: `**The ad in brief.** A 1976 ad for Consumers Hero, a Sugarman company that bought manufacturers' defective returns, refurbished them, and offered them at deep discounts to members of a $5 club. The headline is one word: "HOT." The subheadline offers stolen merchandise to anyone willing to take a risk, and a bold block promises the goods can't be traced to their previous brand or owner. The copy explains the "stealing" concept: inflation robs consumers, so Consumers Hero steals from rich manufacturers and gives to the poor consumer. Then it follows one clock radio through the chain: what it costs to make, what the retailer pays, Uncle Henry returning his broken one, the tiny price Consumers Hero pays, the repair and testing, the five-year warranty, and the members' price. It closes with how the club works, how scarce items are shared fairly, and a membership guarantee.

**What Sugarman points out:**
1. **One of his best slippery slides.** Read the headline, subheadline and bold guarantee and you have to read the first sentence. Start reading and you can't stop until the end (Axiom 6). Chapter 8 uses it as a main slippery slide example, and Chapter 10 cites it for seeds of curiosity, because you read the whole ad before you reach the punch line.
2. **A different voice.** In the JS&A ads he was the space-age expert. Here he's a consumer advocate speaking the consumer's language (trigger #16 Harmonize; Axiom 5). Chapter 7 cites the ad's inflation lines as an example of harmonizing with readers when prices were soaring.
3. **Human without humor** (second quote; trigger #25).
4. **A good copywriter fits any market** (first quote), upscale or downscale, by learning the customer's jargon and words that resonate.

**The same ad in other chapters:**
- **The satisfaction conviction test.** Changing only the guarantee, from a plain partial refund to "never buy anything and we refund your $5 plus interest," doubled response, more than any headline or price change (trigger #9).
- **The name test.** The same ad under the JS&A name far outpulled the Consumers Hero name (trigger #4 Credibility).
- **A bargain environment,** not a gallery (Axiom 4), and folksy member letters that kept the company feeling small (Chapter 15).
- **A grammar complaint.** A reader wrote in about "we better not," a reminder to edit carefully (Chapter 17).

**More to notice:** the cost breakdown is specificity that also explains why the price can be so low (triggers #29, #4). The Robin Hood frame is linking (trigger #14). The concept, not a product, is what's for sale (Axiom 9). And the reasons members are glad he makes a profit come as a triad (copy element #13 Rhythm).`,
      keyPoints: [
        "Headline + subheadline + a bold block can force the first sentence; a strong concept keeps the slide going",
        "Speak the customer's language: same writer, completely different voice for a different market",
        "Show exactly how you can charge so little: specifics make a bargain believable",
        "The satisfaction conviction at the end doubled response in a test",
        "Even the company name affects credibility, so test it"
      ],
      why: "ZDub writes for very different markets in the same week: a premium med spa, a budget-minded HVAC customer, a nervous beginner rider. Consumers Hero proves the principles stay fixed while the voice, jargon and environment change completely.",
